  "slug": "evolutionary-rescue-adaptation-0377",
  "summary": "New preprints link genomic architecture to evolutionary rescue, extinction and invasion, and test how well genomic predictions capture adaptive limits.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how genomic architecture and contingency determine whether populations adapt fast enough to persist. In cowpea seed beetles, rapid, repeatable rescue on lentil relies on large non-recombining haploblocks on chromosomes 1 and 9. In outdoor Drosophila under insecticide, half of the populations went extinct despite strong parallel polygenic adaptation, while pre-adaptation prevented extinction. Copepod salinity adaptation shows polygenic divergence along the Baltic gradient, with genomic offsets limited as predictors of physiological range. Boechera stricta analyses suggest projected hot, dry climates may exceed adaptive capacity. Drosophila suzukii invasion is tested for seasonal adaptive tracking, and stickleback introductions are predicted better by source population identity than by environmental matching. Simulations add that reproductive competition reduces genetic load. New here is the joint weighing of structural variation, polygenic parallelism, contingency and predictive limits.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
